.TH Arkanoid 3
.XI Arkanoid - "\*(mS version of the bat and ball wall game"
.SH NAME
Arkanoid \- \*(mS version of the bat and ball wall game
.Me $a
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
\fBArkanoid\fR
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
Knock  down  the wall of bricks with the ball,  keeping  the ball in play with
the bat  along  the  bottom  of the  screen.  Clear  every  brick  and the next
wall is  loaded.  Let the last ball fall past the bat and a life is lost; lose
them all and the game is over.
.PP
The  bat is  steered  with the  cursor  keys or with  the  mouse,  whichever
was used last.  Where the ball  strikes  the bat  decides  where it goes \- a
hit out  towards  either  end sends the ball away at a shallow  angle,  a hit
near the  middle  sends it back  steeply.  The  speed is the same  whichever
part of the bat is used.
.PP
There are eight walls,  which  repeat once all eight have been  cleared.  The
ball  starts a little  faster on each new level and  creeps  faster  still the
longer a single  wall  takes,  so the later  levels  are  considerably  quicker
than the early ones.  The  current  speed is shown on the status line.
.PP
Bricks  drawn as solid  blocks  cannot be  destroyed  and are not  counted;
they  simply  deflect  the ball.  White  bricks  take two hits,  darkening
after the first.  Every other brick takes one hit.
.PP
A brick  scores  between 60 and 110  points  depending  on its  colour;  a
white  brick  scores 50 for the first hit and 100 for the  second.  A good
capsule  is worth 200 and a bad one 25.  Clearing  the wall scores 500 times
the level number.
.SH CAPSULES
Destroying a brick sometimes  releases a capsule which drifts down the screen.
Catch it with the bat to collect it.  Capsules  \fBstack\fR:  a second  \fBD\fR
splits  every ball again, a second  \fBE\fR  widens the bat  further,  a second
\fBM\fR or \fBG\fR adds to the time  remaining,  and  \fBE\fR and  \fBR\fR wind
the bat back and forth  against  each other.  Everything  collected  lasts until
the ball is lost or the wall is cleared.
.PP
The good capsules are:
.IP \fBE\fR
Expand the bat.
.IP \fBC\fR
Catch \- the ball sticks to the bat until \fBspace\fR is pressed.
.IP \fBL\fR
Laser bat \- \fBspace\fR fires.
.IP \fBS\fR
Slow the ball down.
.IP \fBD\fR
Split every ball in play into three.
.IP \fBP\fR
An extra life.
.IP \fBM\fR
Mega ball \- smashes straight through the wall without bouncing.
.IP \fBG\fR
A guard along the floor, for a while.
.IP \fBT\fR
Twin bat \- two shorter bats with a gap down the middle.
.IP \fBB\fR
Break out of the level immediately.  Rare.
.PP
The bad capsules are all drawn in red:
.IP \fBR\fR
Reduce the bat.
.IP \fBF\fR
Speed the ball up.
.IP \fBV\fR
Reverse the bat controls, for a while.
.SH KEYS
The keys used to control \fBArkanoid\fR are:
.IP "\fBleft\fR or \fBright\fR"
Move the bat.
.IP \fBmouse\fR
The bat will follow your column position of the mouse.
.IP \fBspace\fR
Launch the ball, release a held ball, or fire the laser.
.IP "\fBbutton 1\fR"
The same as \fBspace\fR.
.IP \fBp\fR
Pause the game.
.IP "\fB?\fR or \fBh\fR"
Show the key help.
.IP \fB>\fR
Skip the level, for those who want to see the end.
.IP "\fBq\fR or \fBC-g\fR"
Quit the game.
.SH NOTES
\fBArkanoid\fR is a macro defined in \fCarkanoid.emf\fR.
.PP
The game uses  whatever  room the window  gives it and adjusts if the window
is resized  while  playing.  If the window is too small to lay out a wall the
game  declines to start and says so on the message line.
